====1958–1978: Early appearances====
Okaiken participated in the inaugural [[1958 AFA World Cup]], one of the few East Fosian teams to qualify for the tournament's first edition. The experience was humbling—the Mighty Warriors were eliminated in the group stage with one draw and two defeats, outscored 7–2 across three matches. The early AFA World Cup tournaments exposed the significant gap between Sanese football and the established Fosian and Illypnian powers.

Okaiken missed the next two editions (1962, 1966) before returning at the [[1970 AFA World Cup]], where they again exited at the group stage despite an improved showing that included a first World Cup victory. The [[1978 AFA World Cup]] saw another group-stage exit, though two draws represented a more competitive showing than in previous campaigns.

====1982: Hosts and fourth place====
{{Main|1982 AFA World Cup}}
The [[1982 AFA World Cup]] was a watershed moment for Sanese football. Okaiken was awarded hosting rights in 1978, championed by Prime Minister [[Yasuhiro Nakasone]], who positioned the tournament as a showcase for the nation's modernization under the Republican government. Twelve stadiums across twelve host cities were prepared, including the newly constructed [[Tenji Park Olympic Stadium]] in [[Okami]] with a capacity of 80,000.

As hosts, Okaiken automatically qualified and achieved their best World Cup result to date. The Mighty Warriors advanced through the first group stage and the second group stage to reach the semi-finals, where they were eliminated. In the third-place match, Okaiken fell 2–0 to [[Sanrobonia]] to finish fourth. The tournament—won by [[Balisca]], who defeated [[Asikyira]] 3–1 in the final—was a landmark event, occurring at the dawn of the [[Gogo 8090]] economic boom and showcasing Okaiken's transformation following the 1968 Quiet Revolution. The infrastructure investments, particularly Tenji Park Olympic Stadium, would serve Sanese football for decades.

Despite the on-field success, the period between 1982 and 1998 was a barren one for World Cup qualification. Okaiken failed to qualify for the 1986, 1990, and 1994 tournaments, a drought that reflected the growing competitiveness of AFA qualifying and the slow pace of institutional reform within the OFA.

====1998–2006: Return and rebuilding====
Okaiken ended their 16-year World Cup drought at the [[1998 AFA World Cup]], the result of a breakthrough qualifying campaign under manager [[Ryuichi Taniguchi]]. Drawn into a difficult group, the Mighty Warriors showed promise but lacked the depth to advance, drawing one match and losing two. The return to the World Cup was a milestone in itself, confirming that the OFA's institutional reforms were beginning to bear fruit.

Okaiken reached the knockout stage for the first time at the [[2002 AFA World Cup]], advancing from the group stage before falling in the round of 16. The campaign was marked by a disciplined defensive performance and a counter-attacking style that frustrated more technically gifted opponents. The [[2006 AFA World Cup]] represented a step back—a group-stage exit that prompted a tactical reassessment and the beginning of the OFA's investment in youth development infrastructure.

====2010: Quarter-final breakthrough====
The [[2010 AFA World Cup]] saw Okaiken reach the quarter-finals for the first time since their 1982 fourth-place finish as hosts, a result that signaled the team's return to the upper echelon of international football. A physically imposing squad, bolstered by the emerging generation of players who would later define the 2014 championship run, pushed a favored opponent to the limit before being eliminated. The tournament was also the World Cup debut of a young [[Keisuke Honda]], who announced himself on the global stage with a goal and an assist in the group stage.

====2014: Champions of Adonia====
{{Main|2014 AFA World Cup}}
The [[2014 AFA World Cup]] in [[Varkana]] was the defining moment in Sanese football history. Okaiken entered the tournament as a dark horse—a team with talent and physicality but without the pedigree of the traditional Fosian and Illypnian powerhouses. Under the tactical guidance of manager [[Stefan Maurice]], who had taken over the team in 2012, the Mighty Warriors adopted a ferocious ''Gegenpressing'' system that overwhelmed opponents with relentless physical intensity.

'''Group stage:''' Okaiken were drawn into Group D with [[Balisca]], [[Nakamizu]], and [[Dagit]]. The opening match against Balisca was a statement of intent—[[Keisuke Honda]] opened the scoring with a 30-meter free kick in the 23rd minute and added two assists as Okaiken won 3–1. The second match against Nakamizu was tighter, with Honda scoring once and providing an assist in a 2–1 victory. The final group match against Dagit was a 4–1 demolition, with Honda scoring twice, that confirmed Okaiken as group winners with a perfect record.

'''Round of 16:''' Okaiken faced a physical [[Bierland]] side in the knockout round. The match was a bruising affair, with both teams trading heavy challenges. [[Tadaoki Kawabe]] scored the winner in the 67th minute, pouncing on a defensive error forced by the pressing system to slot home from 12 meters. Okaiken advanced 1–0.

'''Quarter-finals:''' The quarter-final against [[Ayori]] tested Okaiken's tactical discipline. Ayori's possession-based style threatened to bypass the press, but Maurice adjusted at halftime, switching from a 3-4-3 to a more compact 3-5-2 that clogged the central lanes. Honda scored from a set piece in the 58th minute, and Kawabe added a second on the counter-attack. Okaiken won 2–0.

'''Semi-finals:''' The semi-final against [[Sansi]] was the most grueling match of the tournament. Sansi's defensive discipline neutralized Okaiken's pressing for long stretches, and the match went to extra time tied 1–1 after Honda's opener was cancelled out by a Sansi equalizer. In extra time, Honda drove a low left-footed shot through a crowd of defenders from 22 meters in the 112th minute to win 2–1 and send Okaiken to the final.

'''Final:''' The final against [[Volisania]] was decided in extra time. Volisania took the lead in the 67th minute, and the match seemed lost until Honda equalized in the 84th minute, driving a low left-footed shot through a crowd of defenders from 22 meters. In extra time, Honda provided the assist for Kawabe's winner—a perfectly weighted through ball that split the Volisanian defense. Okaiken prevailed 2–1 after extra time.

Honda finished the tournament with 9 goals and 5 assists in 7 matches, earning both the Golden Ball (tournament's best player) and the Golden Boot (top scorer). The victory propelled Okaiken to the number one spot in the AFA World Rankings for the first time in the nation's history.

====2018: Title defense====
The [[2018 AFA World Cup]] in [[Ayorin]] was a bitter disappointment. The core of the 2014 championship squad had aged, and the generational transition was not yet complete. Okaiken advanced from the group stage with a win and two draws but were eliminated in the round of 16 by a faster, more dynamic opponent. The defeat prompted a comprehensive review of the OFA's youth development pipeline and accelerated the integration of the next generation of players under the [[National Iron Body Initiative]].

====2022: Return to the quarter-finals====
The [[2022 AFA World Cup]] in [[Sansi]] marked Okaiken's return to competitiveness. A new generation of players—most notably captain [[Takeru Iwano]] and vice-captain [[Issei Kawada]]—had matured into the team's spine, combining the technical ability of their predecessors with even greater physical intensity.

'''Group stage:''' Okaiken topped their group with two wins and a draw, conceding only one goal. The defensive unit, anchored by Iwano and [[Kazuki Ota]], was the tournament's most impenetrable in the group stage.

'''Round of 16:''' Okaiken faced a stubborn opponent in the knockout round. Kawabe scored the opener in the first half, and Kawada sealed the result with a long-range strike in the 78th minute after winning possession through the press. Okaiken advanced 2–0.

'''Quarter-finals:''' The quarter-final was a war of attrition that went to penalties after a 1–1 draw through extra time. Iwano was immense in defense, winning every aerial duel and organizing the back line under sustained pressure, but Okaiken's composure deserted them in the shootout. Two missed penalties ended the campaign. Despite the loss, the tournament established Iwano as the spiritual successor to the 2014 generation and confirmed that Okaiken's rebuild was on track.

====2026: Group stage winners, Round of 16 exit====
The [[2026 AFA World Cup]], co-hosted by [[Balisca]] and [[Sanrobonia]], marked Okaiken's eighth consecutive World Cup appearance. The Mighty Warriors qualified as the dominant force in East Fosia qualifying, topping their regional group with a near-perfect record. The team entered the tournament ranked 7th in the AFA World Rankings, with a squad that blended World Cup-winning experience ([[Keisuke Honda]], Kawabe) with emerging talent.

'''Group stage:''' Okaiken were drawn into Group E with [[Bayan]] and [[Sanyan]]. The Mighty Warriors opened with a 2–1 victory over Bayan on 15 June, followed by a 1–1 draw with Sanyan on 18 June. On Matchday 5, Bayan and Sanyan drew 2–2, confirming Okaiken as Group E winners on 4 points without playing. The group stage showcased the defensive partnership of Iwano and Ota, while [[Kenzo Sato]]—the goalkeeper—emerged as an unlikely goalscorer, netting Okaiken's opening goal in the knockout round.

'''Round of 16:''' Okaiken faced [[Ayorin]], the Group C runner-up, on 27 June at Arena Yerba in Yerba Buena—the same venue that had hosted the Mighty Warriors' camp for three weeks. Kenzo Sato gave Okaiken the lead in the 42nd minute. Ayorin equalised in the 60th. In the first minute of stoppage time, Ayorin struck the winner. Final: 1–2. Coach Maurice: "That is football. I am proud of every player." Captain Honda: "We go home with our heads up." The team departed Balisca on 28 June. #StillWeRise trended within minutes of the defeat—the tone shifting from celebration to farewell.

The tournament was widely viewed as a disappointment relative to Okaiken's 7th-ranked pedigree and group-winning performance. The Mighty Warriors went unbeaten in the group stage but lost in the first knockout round to a team that had finished its group on 1 point. The result prompted renewed scrutiny of Maurice's tactical approach in knockout matches and the team's inability to convert group-stage dominance into knockout-stage progression.

